{"number":12,"title":"Factorisation","slug":"factorisation","color":"","level":"","estTime":"","description":"Class 8 IMO — Factorisation: taking out common factors, factorising by regrouping, using identities, splitting the middle term of quadratic trinomials, and dividing algebraic expressions.","topics":[{"title":"Common Factors & Regrouping","concept":"<p>To factorise, take out the <strong>highest common factor</strong> first: 6x + 9 = 3(2x + 3). When terms have no single common factor, group them: ax + ay + bx + by = a(x + y) + b(x + y) = (a + b)(x + y).</p>","visual":null,"examples":[{"number":"1","problem":"Factorise 6x + 9.","solution":"3(2x + 3)."},{"number":"2","problem":"Factorise ax + ay + bx + by.","solution":"(a + b)(x + y)."}],"recap":["Take out the highest common factor first.","Use regrouping when there is no single common factor."],"quiz":[{"q":"Factorise 4x + 8.","options":["4(x + 2)","2(2x + 8)","4(x + 8)","x(4 + 8)"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The HCF is 4: 4(x + 2).","section":"mathematical-reasoning","difficulty":"easy"},{"q":"Factorise x² + x.","options":["x(x + 1)","x²(1 + 1)","(x + 1)","x(x − 1)"],"correct":0,"explanation":"x is common: x(x + 1).","section":"mathematical-reasoning","difficulty":"easy"}]},{"title":"Factorisation Using Identities","concept":"<p>Identities help factorise: <strong>a² − b² = (a + b)(a − b)</strong> and <strong>a² + 2ab + b² = (a + b)²</strong>. So x² − 9 = (x + 3)(x − 3) and x² + 6x + 9 = (x + 3)².</p>","visual":null,"examples":[{"number":"1","problem":"Factorise x² − 9.","solution":"(x + 3)(x − 3)."},{"number":"2","problem":"Factorise x² + 6x + 9.","solution":"(x + 3)²."}],"recap":["a² − b² = (a + b)(a − b).","a² + 2ab + b² = (a + b)²."],"quiz":[{"q":"Factorise x² − 25.","options":["(x + 5)(x − 5)","(x − 5)²","(x + 5)²","(x + 25)(x − 1)"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Difference of squares: (x + 5)(x − 5).","section":"mathematical-reasoning","difficulty":"medium"},{"q":"Factorise x² − 16.","options":["(x + 4)(x − 